Critical appreciation on a quote from Slyvia Plath

"What horrifies me the most
is the idea of being useless:
well educated, brilliantly
promised, and fading out into
an indifferent middle age."

Slyvia Plath was a brilliant american novel writter, who
fell into severe depression during her time in college,
which is what fueled her to write this quote. This quote
explains what she is afraid of and why she is afraid of it.
Her mind fell into a deep hole that she couldn't escape 
because she felt like studying was making her ability to 
write so creatively.

"What horrifies me the most is the idea of being useless"
these words tell us that the idea of her being useless 
was the thing that she feared worried that her ability to
write was useless. The next words describes more of what 
she is afraid of. When she says "Well educated" she means
she's afraid what will happen when she studies so much that
her great ability to write will be lost. "Brilliantly promised"
tells us she's scared of not living up to expectations and 
feeling worthless when she dosen't. The last few words indicates
what she thinks will happen when she tries hard to be perfect 
just becasue someone set expectations for her.

This quote shows how much a person really tries,when no one
is there, to reach a peak of a mountain just to be seen. 
Proving that sometimes a person needs a break from life 
and live their fullest by doing what they truely desire.
Never put expectations on people because you don't realise 
what goes on in their dark world.